PRIVATE REFLECTION: {reflection}

The public responses split mainly between C (correctly estimate) and B (underestimate), with one A (overestimate). The majority (1,3,4,6) give the standard derivation: an all-or-nothing vaccine protects a fraction \(p\) completely; the remaining \(1-p\) have the same per-exposure risk as unvaccinated. Thus the vaccinated incidence rate is \((1-p)\) times the unvaccinated rate, so \(IRR = 1-p\) and \(1-IRR = p\). If \(p\) is interpreted as the average per-exposure vaccine efficacy across vaccinees, then C is correct. This assumes comparable exposure, equal follow-up, and no substantial depletion of susceptibles.

Key competing arguments:
- Colleague 2 (A) argues that protected individuals never become infected but still contribute person-time, lowering the vaccinated incidence rate more than the true average per-exposure reduction, so \(1-IRR\) overestimates \(p\). This is a real bias if IRR is computed over long follow-up with depletion of susceptibles; then \(IRR < 1-p\), so \(1-IRR > p\). However, it does not hold under the standard rare-infection/equal-person-time assumption used by the C responses.
- Colleagues 5 and 7 (B) argue that \(1-IRR = p < 1\), so it underestimates the per-exposure efficacy among protected individuals, which is 100%. This treats “per-exposure vaccine efficacy” as conditional on being a responder/protected, rather than the population-average efficacy. That is a nonstandard target unless the question explicitly defines it that way.

Current leaning: C for the intended standard interpretation: \(1-IRR\) estimates the proportion protected, which is the average per-exposure vaccine efficacy for an all-or-nothing vaccine. Important caveat: if the question is probing the incidence-rate/person-time bias specifically, A could be correct; if it defines per-exposure VE conditionally among protected individuals, B could be correct. The main unresolved issue is the precise definition of “per-exposure vaccine efficacy” and whether IRR is assumed to be over a long follow-up with depletion.}

